  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Wynnea sparassoides is a small, inconspicuous fungus in the Wynneaceae family native to New England forests. It is a wood-decaying saprotroph that plays an important ecological role in nutrient cycling within forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Sparassoid structure",
      "description": "Characterized by its distinctive branching pattern resembling a spider-like or crab-like appearance, which is reflected in its species name 'sparassoides'",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size and coloration",
      "description": "Typically appears as a small, dark, brownish to black fruiting body that is often difficult to spot among leaf litter",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Substrate preference",
      "description": "Found exclusively on decaying hardwood logs and woody debris, particularly in moist microhabitats",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Wynnea sparassoides thrives in moist deciduous and mixed forests of New England, preferring shaded forest floors with abundant decaying wood and high humidity.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its small and often overlooked appearance, Wynnea sparassoides is an important decomposer that helps break down woody material, recycling essential nutrients back into the forest soil.",
